**Read:** [Sedimentary Geology — John Southard, MIT 12.110](../sources/MIT-Sedimentary-Geology.lingua#r_7FWZZ66CT5G1FP2JPQMWRV1YHM), all siliciclastic and carbonate diagenesis notes; [Guide to Thin Section Microscopy — Raith, Raase, and Reinhardt](../sources/Guide-to-Thin-Section-Microscopy.lingua#r_2MH30T4MGW8YMVP9SA3Z7XCDSD), the full relevant texture/measurement sections; [Petrology — Dexter Perkins, open textbook](../sources/Petrology-Perkins.lingua#r_6P7JBVJJ3P1BT6QKDGC7BN6VWV), the complete relevant alteration/reaction treatment.

Diagenesis changes sediment/rock after deposition through physical, chemical, and biological processes under an identified history. Compaction, dissolution, cementation, replacement, recrystallization, and authigenesis can interact. A convenient boundary between diagenesis and metamorphism is descriptive/contextual, not a claim that the natural processes stop abruptly at one universal temperature.

Separate porosity amount, pore geometry/connectivity, and permeability. Cement can fill pores or stabilize a framework; dissolution can create pores without establishing a connected flow network. A thin-section image is a selected two-dimensional observation requiring complementary measurements for many flow/engineering claims.

Cross-cutting cements, overgrowths, grain contacts, replacement fronts, inclusions, and chemistry can order events. A textural sequence needs age/temperature/fluid constraints before becoming a quantitative burial history. Later fluids can overprint earlier signals selectively rather than reset every phase together.

**Work:** reconstruct a documented diagenetic sequence from the full methods and observations, distinguishing direct relationships, inferred stages, and unresolved ordering. Compare two fluid/burial histories and test a consequential mass-balance or porosity claim. Explain the additional measurements required to connect the interpretation with groundwater flow, storage, aggregate durability, or another material use.

Read the complete siliciclastic and carbonate diagenesis notes. The advanced sedimentary project will require a sustained history rather than a list of recognized cements.

